From: Anders Sandberg <nv91-asa@nada.kth.se>
Date: Thu, 6 Oct 1994 11:47:58 +0100 (MET)
Subject: [#94-10-100] [#94-10-81] Uploads...

Jason  Hamilton wrote:

>Why would one want to upload oneself?

To become immortal. To be able to create back-up copies. To be able to
exist faster at faster hardware. To be able to modify oneself easier. To
be able to branch off, each copy solving different problems. Possibilities
of great cognitive (and emotional) enchancements. Generally, software is 
much more flexible than wetware. 


>Is it the same urge that drives people to have chlidren?  Your copy 
>personality is, for all observers, basically the same person as you.  I 
>suppose for people who have children in order to pass on a bit of 
>themselves, an uploaded personality would be the ideal child.  Is that it?

This depends a bit on how much you care about preserving your identity. 
Some (perhaps most?) people interested in uploading themselves want a 
fairly similar copy (I prefer the word "successor entity" from Norman 
Spinrad's Deus X), which can be said to constitute themselves (this is 
especially true for destructive uploading). Others (like me) feel that 
the successor entities doesn't have to be direct translations of our 
mental processes, just carry on the important and interesting bits. 

I think some people would try to create modified uploads from the
beginning, not seeing them as an extension of themselves but as "children"
(see for example the description of the creation of Tapio in "Eternity" by
Greg Bear). However, most of the impetus for uploading is a longing for 
immortality, personal freedom and growth.


>Because, if not, then I have to wonder about the technique.  If you 
>actually want it to be YOU, then the upload technique will have to do 
>more than simply copy you perfectly.
>
>I have a feeling that this may have already been discussed.  If so, I'm 
>sorry for dropping in halfway through. 

The identity problem is an old question, which have been endlessly 
debated. I usually get bored with such discussions after a while, since 
they seldom lead to any new ideas. I prefer to wait and see if my upload 
is me or somebody else. 


