From: Anders Sandberg <nv91-asa@nada.kth.se>
Date: Thu, 6 Oct 1994 12:24:17 +0100 (MET)
Subject: [#94-10-101] Mental Changes

Derek Ryan's description of his conversion was interesting. It implies
that our mental frameworks can shift quite dramatically in a very short
time, apparently caused by very small external stimuli if the internal
"groundwork" has been laid out. I've seen references to roughly similar
events in other places, where sudden profound changes in personality or
outlook occur. 

There is even a Swedish idiom ("Den Lidnerska Kn{ppen") based upon such a
case, where a rather ordinary pupil suffered some sort of quick stroke and
then became a very good learner and later author.  They seem to be linked
to periods of stress or memetic opposition, quite often hidden from the
conscious mental layers, which suddenly can undergo total changes (often
interpreted in a religious way). 

Some notes: 

>1)  Right before (literally *minutes* before) I stopped believing, I 
>was at my peak as a believer.  I had never had so much faith, never 
>been so active, and never been so able to defend belief on an 
>intellectual level.

Perhaps this state was partially the cause of the conversion. It denied
that there were any chance of doubt, and thus forced the atheist memes
into the subconscious, but at the same time it did not provide a very good
memetic defense, since it was too convinced that it was invulnerable
(sounds like the Maginot-line). 

>And what a great study in the complexity of "mind."

Indeed. It would be wonderful if we could learn how to use such phenomena
in a constructive way. Imagine being able to completely modify our
personalities or belief to suit the situation. Unfortunately, so far they
seem to be linked to intense mental or emotional stress in most cases,
making them a bit unsafe ("Oops, the psychoprogrammer made a small error
and now I'm a devout fundamentalist!"). Perhaps we should learn more about
the mechanism and then modify it? 


